    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm reading up on some Cessna flight manuals and I'm a bit confused. Can you explain exactly how a pitot tube actually works to measure airspeed? Like, what is the physics behind the pressure difference?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A pitot tube works on Bernoulli's principle, which states that total pressure equals static pressure plus dynamic pressure. The tube faces directly into the airflow, capturing the full total (stagnation) pressure as the air is brought to a stop at its opening, while small static ports elsewhere on the aircraft sense the ambient static pressure. The airspeed indicator measures the difference between these two pressures—this dynamic pressure is proportional to the square of the airspeed, which is why the instrument's mechanism converts it into a readable speed. That's why pitot heat matters in icing conditions too: a blocked pitot tube can leave you with unreliable airspeed indications.",
      "source": "target",
