    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so if the tube gets blocked it messes up the reading. i read somewhere that even small things like bugs getting inside can cause issues. how common is that actually? like, what kind of stuff usually causes a blockage in those small tubes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Good instinct—the infamous \"Birgenair Flight 301\" crash in 1996 traced back to an insect nesting in a pitot tube on a grounded aircraft, which is a great example of how a tiny obstruction can be catastrophic. Beyond bugs, the main culprits are ice (which is why pitot heat exists), insects and their nests (especially during Layovers in tropical or humid regions), and contamination from things like dust, dirt, or water ingress. That's weird capitalization in there—odd. Maintenance crews install pitot covers when parked, and regular inspections catch most issues, so it's genuinely rare in practice. But since blockage behavior differs depending on whether the pitot, the static port, or both are blocked, it's worth understanding all those failure cases.",
